    <link type="text/html" href="https://dbdo00.github.io/notes/journals/2026_11_15.html" />
    <title>2026_11_15</title>
    <published>2026-11-15T10:05:18Z</published>
    <updated>2026-11-17T12:57:46Z</updated>
    <content type="html">&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Q: What is the largest number of points on the plain, each two of which have the equal &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$\ell_1$&lt;/span&gt; distance?
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$x \in B(y)$&lt;/span&gt;, then &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$B(x) \cap B(y) \leq 2$&lt;/span&gt;. Where &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$B()$&lt;/span&gt; is the &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$\ell_1$&lt;/span&gt; ball of the plane.
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$x,\ y$&lt;/span&gt;  have \ell 1 distance &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$d$&lt;/span&gt;, what is the number of other points having the equal distance from &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$x$&lt;/span&gt; &lt;strong&gt;and&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$y$&lt;/span&gt;?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The answer is &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$2$&lt;/span&gt;, as the inequality above asserts.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;So, the maximal number of pairwise &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$\ell_1$&lt;/span&gt;-equidistant points on &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$\mathbb R^2$&lt;/span&gt; is &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$\boxed 4$&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
