    <link type="text/html" href="https://dbdo00.github.io/notes/journals/2025_12_15.html" />
    <title>2025_12_15</title>
    <published>2025-12-15T06:34:57Z</published>
    <updated>2026-11-17T12:57:46Z</updated>
    <content type="html">&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;determinant: &lt;em&gt;adding sides method&lt;/em&gt; to compute determinants.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;calculus: intermediate value theorem. For the linear type of questions with constant coefficients, we can construct the function just like we do in for ODE.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Abelian Fundamental Group: the isomorphism map of isomorphic fundamental groups, in this case, has only one  possibility --
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;suppose &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$x$&lt;/span&gt; and &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$y$&lt;/span&gt; are two base points, and &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$\alpha$&lt;/span&gt; and &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$\alpha^\prime$&lt;/span&gt; are two paths connecting &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$x$&lt;/span&gt; and &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$y$&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;In this case, &lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$[\alpha(\alpha^\prime)^{-1}] \in \pi(y)$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;math inline&quot;&gt;$\alpha \circ {\alpha^\prime}^{-1} = id_{\pi(y)} \implies \alpha = \alpha^\prime$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
</content>
